Date:     Mon, 6 Dec 93 15:00:46 EST
From: cards@top.cis.syr.edu
Subject:  Re:  TECH: What I would do with 10 mpbs IP connection

Glen Daniels wonders how much bandwidth a channel has.
That depends upon what is meant by 'bandwidth'.

A TV channel is 6 MHz wide in frequency, edge to edge.
Generally it is necessary to leave some margin to either
side of the frequency range which you actually intend to use
to carry information.  In the proposed HDTV systems, some
proposers plan to shave this margin to as little as 300 kHz
on each side, using perhaps as much as 5.4 MHz to carry
information.

Then the number of bits per second which you can cram
through the passband depends upon your pulse shape
and modulation technique, which are constrained by the
signal to noise ratio of the channel.  A pulse shape
with a bandwidth efficiency of 50% is easily achieved,
yielding 2.7 Mbaud.  64-level quadrature amplitude
modulation is widely proposed for HDTV transmission,
and provides 6 bits per baud.  This leads to a
'bandwidth' in raw bps terms of 16.2Mbps.

Then you have to consider the efficiency of the whole
protocol stack, which will likely be below 50%,
giving you at best 8 Mbps of payload throughput.
Don't forget about error detection or correction
(I have assumed reasonable levels of overhead
for this in the protocol efficiency).

With a sufficiently high S/N ratio, there is no limit
on the bps which can be crammed through any finite
bandwidth.  Realistic S/N figures for metropolitan
area CATV systems limit us to 10Mbps as a rough
order of magnitude estimate on actual throughput.

the comm engineers out there know I am simplifying
and biasing the estimates with my personal experiences
with S/N; other folks may have encountered much
better or worse conditions on other cable systems

