Date: Mon, 6 Dec 93 12:35:26 PST
From: peb@procase.com (Paul Baclace)
Subject: TECH: Fluorescent/Incandescent lighting query

Fluorescent lights are horrible; I've had all the bulbs removed from 
around my office.  They can give me headaches and stomach pain, but
I'm a bit photosensitive.  I always wear sunglasses in grocery 
stores, etc. 

The spectrum of fluorescents isn't appealing and they flash rapidly
(peripheral vision can detect this better than the fovea).  Although
there are tri-phosphor bulbs that can appear to be more like sunlight,
they actually produce a series of narrow frequencies rather than a 
smooth thermal emission like an incandescent.  That's my theory about
why I don't like fluorescents; incandescents and halogen lights never
bother me.

Fluorescents are used to get people to move fast: notice how all fast
food resturants use them (and 7-11's are pretty bright too).  These
businesses profit more from fast turnover.  On the other hand, a
resturant that profits more by having people linger--say, one that
sells high-prices drinks, wants subdued, pleasant lighting to keep you
around.  The ubiquity of businesses using this lighting selection is a
strong indication of its effectiveness: fluorescent is annoying, 
incandescent and halogen are pleasant.


