I'd like to detail further on browsing versus list-shopping:

1) Shopping malls are, in my experience, a north-american phenomenon,
which parking problems do not fully account for.

[ for the picky:  Yes, there are huge French shopping malls but they
tend to be extensions of downtown shopping areas, full of small
independant stores, as opposed of piles of chain stores.
And yes too, the French huge grocery/hardware/books/anything
"hypermarches", which live in the suburbs, tend to
attract a few neighboring specialized stores (only
american equivalent to these huge stores may be WalMart. They are
definitely retail stores, not like Price/Costco) ... And don't tell
me that there is no parking problem in France ]

So, with the parking/zoning reason eliminated:

Would stores in VR be gathered together in malls? Or would they be
part of the parks, affluent downtowns, and other specific meeting
points (the MUD middle of town fountain square?) Why not in the MUD's
sewer for some fashion stores?
Would there be virtual "zoning"? why? How much would the city planners
influence the development, once they have defined a general plan (and
actually built it :-), why should the actual users be prevented from
messing around with it? Wouldn't that actually make the city live,
evolve, feel like a real Place? On the other hand how do you make a
street keep looking like a street day after day if anybody can go
ahead and build stuff anywhere (including more store fronts below the
street level, and blimps floating above the street itself)?

Real-estate is cheap in VR, it would make sense to have store fronts
placed here and there, all connected to the same store (or
salesperson, soft or wet)? (you know who your customers are when they
insist on walking in from the sewer entrance, instead of the suburban
mall.)

2) People do not shop with a list in north america because
it does not work:

The malls have all the same chain stores that
distribute the same stuff. You have to buy what they
have. I still shop with lists, simply it takes months to
find each item :-) When I go to a mall (for all the good reasons
mentioned by Doug) it's pretty clear from the outset that I won't
make much progress on my list.

When you want a specific book, do you browse the bookstore
at random, or go straight to the shelf, then to a salesdroid
(where could the term come from??? the interaction is not
always that good... sometimes it is), finally, when you have
the book, you can browse and maybe get something else. So,
goal-oriented shopping does work with bookstores (even there,
not always). But browsing and list shopping can be beneficially
separated. Browsing in the chain bookstores is usually pretty
disappointing to me (except huge ones like Barnes and Noble)...
because again they all have the same boring stuff.

3) magazines and catalogs:

A glossy magazine or catalog is not a shopping medium. It's a
browsing medium. It has been carefully edited to appeal to
some categuory of people. It is the result of the work of people
that have done a lot of browsing themselves and have condensed
their experience there. It is pointers.  In this process,
real-time interaction is counter-productive. Let them
do their work alone, you will get the result faster.
Then send feedback.

Most current catalogs that show one to six items of each type, to me,
are the same as magazines, just less interesting to browse, just as
inefficient for shopping.

Conclusion:

List shopping would work if you knew of places that have "every book"
or "every style of silverware", etc... And furthermore have it at the
right price. It's only a small part of their job to let you know what
exists out there. But if you can describe it, and it exists, they can
get it for you. Here too, real-time interaction is counter-productive.

All the social interaction that Doug described, does not have to happen
in malls. Indeed it's pretty pityful when the only place it can happen
is shopping malls... Let's not reproduce that on the net, Please? IRC,
and game muds, and discussion lists like this one are much more lively
places, together with the real life meetings that follow. The first two
do benefit from real-time interaction.

Browsing is yet a third function. But fashion magazines are probably
the best medium for that currently, if it were not for their price,
and lack of usable pointers (local store names).

If you want to provide for more impulse buying, just make sure it's
possible, when you cross somebody in VR, to get a pointer to where
they got their head from, without disturbing them (or maybe by asking
them), and then make sure they get some incentive
money from the head store for their trouble and good advertising. 


