would be unable to integrate these three titles. To me, it is apparent
that gender differences are not only older than our generation, they're
older than our *species*, and they permeate our entire culture. (By
"our culture," I mean the predominant culture of those of us who share
this list: Western Civilization. However, these differences certainly
permeate the other non-western cultures as well.)

Obviously, when one talks about gender differences, we're talking about
general patterns that are true in the aggregate and don't necessarily
apply to single individuals. I believe that, on average, men and women
approach life in different ways and have different skill sets. However,
that doesn't mean that all women are more concerned with inter-personal
relationships than all men, or that all men are better at spacial
relations problems than all women, to use two common examples.

In addition, I think that sometimes, too much is made of the
differences because people fail to examine the underlying motives.
You'll often hear that female friends are closer because they talk
about relationships, whereas male friends are aloof and uninvolved
because they talk about cars or go fishing instead. This view overlooks
the commonality between the two cases (creating bonds) because one way
of doing it seems different from the other. But one person's style is
another person's substance. Just because all levels of an interaction
aren't immediately apparent doesn't mean they aren't there.

Also, I think that many women don't understand the male dominance
hierarchy.  Some don't even perceive it at all. And men largely can't
imagine the absence of the hiearchy. This is certainly a source of
confusion for both genders.

It's also interesting that another poster (I apologize, but I
accidentally deleted the message, so I don't remember who) said they
had seen little sexism against men, or at least very little that
bothered them.

Actually, I think there's quite a bit of sexist talk commonly directed
at men, especially straight, white men, but most people don't pick up
on it, or don't feel as strongly about it as they do about sexist talk
directed at women. As a simple example, try to find a television
commercial in which a husband has any wits at all. Or one in which a
male and female argue, and he "wins" the confrontation. They largely
don't exist.

I'm certainly not saying that this is some kind of crushing oppression
for men, but I find it interesting to note how often opprobrium for
misogyny comes out as unabashed misandry.

